# Operations

Hợp nhất từ Alerting Patterns và Incident Response. Tài liệu này định nghĩa các nguyên tắc và best practices cho việc vận hành hệ thống trong Cursor Enterprise Framework, bao gồm proactive issue detection, incident response, và continuous improvement.

## 1. Alerting Patterns

### 1.1 Alert Severity Levels

Define clear severity levels.

```yaml
# Alert severity levels
severity_levels:
  P1_CRITICAL:
    description: "Complete service outage or data loss"
    response_time: 15 minutes
    notification: Phone call + SMS + Slack
    escalation: Immediate

  P2_HIGH:
    description: "Major functionality impaired"
    response_time: 30 minutes
    notification: SMS + Slack
    escalation: 30 minutes

  P3_MEDIUM:
    description: "Minor functionality affected"
    response_time: 4 hours
    notification: Slack
    escalation: 4 hours

  P4_LOW:
    description: "Cosmetic or non-urgent issue"
    response_time: Next business day
    notification: Email + ticket
```

### 1.2 Alerting Rules

Prometheus-style alerting rules.

```yaml
# prometheus/alerts.yml
groups:
  - name: api-service-alerts
    interval: 30s
    rules:
      # Critical alerts
      - alert: ServiceDown
        expr: up{job="api-service"} == 0
        for: 1m
        labels:
          severity: critical
          team: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "API service is down"
          description: "Instance {{ $labels.instance }} has been down for more than 1 minute"
          runbook_url: "https://wiki.example.com/runbooks/service-down"

      - alert: HighErrorRate
        expr: |
          (
            sum(rate(http_requests_total{status_code=~"5.."}[5m]))
            / sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))
          ) > 0.05
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "High HTTP 5xx error rate"
          description: "Error rate is {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} (threshold: 5%)"
          runbook_url: "https://wiki.example.com/runbooks/high-error-rate"

      # Warning alerts
      - alert: HighLatency
        expr: |
          histogram_quantile(0.95,
            sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])) by (le)
          ) > 1
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "High API latency detected"
          description: "95th percentile latency is {{ $value }}s (threshold: 1s)"

      - alert: HighMemoryUsage
        expr: |
          (container_memory_usage_bytes / container_memory_limit_bytes) > 0.85
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "High memory usage"
          description: "Memory usage at {{ $value | humanizePercentage }}"

      # Database alerts
      - alert: DatabaseConnectionsHigh
        expr: |
          pg_stat_activity_count / pg_settings_max_connections > 0.8
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "High database connection usage"
          description: "Connection usage at {{ $value | humanizePercentage }}"

      - alert: DatabaseSlowQueries
        expr: |
          rate(pg_stat_statements_mean_exec_time[5m]) > 1000
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Slow database queries detected"
          description: "Average query execution time is {{ $value }}ms"
```

### 1.3 SLO-Based Alerts

Alert based on service level objectives.

```yaml
# SLO-based alerting
slo_alerts:
  availability:
    name: "API Availability SLO"
    target: 99.9  # 99.9% availability
    window: 30d
    alert_on_budget_consumption: true
    budget:
      total: 100
      thresholds:
        - percent: 50
          severity: warning
          message: "Availability budget 50% consumed"
        - percent: 75
          severity: critical
          message: "Availability budget 75% consumed - taking action required"
        - percent: 90
          severity: p1
          message: "Availability budget exhausted - immediate action required"

  latency:
    name: "API Latency SLO"
    target: 99  # 99% of requests under 500ms
    window: 30d
    alert_on_budget_consumption: true

# Burn rate alerts
burn_rate_alerts:
  - alert: FastburnAlert
    expr: |
      sum(slo:error-budget-remaining:ratio{slo="api-availability"} < 1)
      and
      (sum(rate(slo:errors-total:slo="api-availability"[1h]))
      / sum(rate(slo:total:slo="api-availability"[1h]))) > 0.1
    for: 15m
    labels:
      severity: critical
    annotations:
      summary: "Fast burn rate detected"
      description: "Error budget is burning fast - 10% of budget in 1 hour"

  - alert: SlowburnAlert
    expr: |
      sum(slo:error-budget-remaining:ratio{slo="api-availability"} < 1)
      and
      (sum(rate(slo:errors-total:slo="api-availability"[6h]))
      / sum(rate(slo:total:slo="api-availability"[6h]))) > 0.01
    for: 1h
    labels:
      severity: warning
```

### 1.4 Alert Routing

Route alerts to appropriate channels.

```yaml
# AlertManager configuration
global:
  resolve_timeout: 5m

route:
  group_by: ['alertname', 'severity', 'service']
  group_wait: 30s
  group_interval: 5m
  repeat_interval: 4h
  receiver: 'default-receiver'
  routes:
    # Critical alerts - immediate phone call
    - match:
        severity: critical
      receiver: 'pagerduty-critical'
      group_wait: 0s
      repeat_interval: 1h

    # Database alerts
    - match:
        component: database
      receiver: 'database-team'
      routes:
        - match:
            severity: critical
          receiver: 'database-oncall'

    # API alerts
    - match:
        component: api
      receiver: 'api-team'
      routes:
        - match:
            alertname: 'ServiceDown'
          receiver: 'pagerduty-critical'

    # Security alerts - dedicated channel
    - match:
        category: security
      receiver: 'security-team'
      routes:
        - match:
            severity: critical
          receiver: 'security-oncall'

receivers:
  - name: 'default-receiver'
    slack_configs:
      - channel: '#alerts-default'
        send_resolved: true

  - name: 'pagerduty-critical'
    pagerduty_configs:
      - service_key: '${PAGERDUTY_SERVICE_KEY}'
        severity: critical
        send_resolved: true
        client: 'Prometheus'
        client_url: '{{ .CommonLabels.alertmanagerUrl }}'

  - name: 'database-team'
    slack_configs:
      - channel: '#alerts-database'
        send_resolved: true

  - name: 'security-oncall'
    slack_configs:
      - channel: '#alerts-security-critical'
    pagerduty_configs:
      - service_key: '${SECURITY_PAGERDUTY_KEY}'
        severity: critical
```

### 1.5 Alert Suppression

Prevent alert storms và noise.

```yaml
# Alert suppression rules
suppression:
  # Suppress during maintenance window
  - name: "maintenance-window"
    condition: |
      maintenance_mode == true
    affected_alerts:
      - "HighMemoryUsage"
      - "HighCPUUsage"
      - "HighLatency"

  # Suppress after auto-scaling
  - name: "auto-scaling-in-progress"
    condition: |
      kube_deployment_spec_replicas > kube_deployment_status_replicas
    affected_alerts:
      - "HighCPUUsage"
      - "HighMemoryUsage"
    duration: 10m

  # Acknowledgement based suppression
  - name: "acknowledged-alerts"
    condition: |
      alert_state == "acknowledged"
    affected_alerts:
      - all
    duration: 1h

# Mute schedules
mute_schedules:
  - name: "weekly-maintenance"
    time_intervals:
      - time_range: "saturday 22:00 - sunday 06:00"
        weekdays: ['saturday']
      - time_range: "sunday 00:00 - sunday 06:00"
        weekdays: ['sunday']

  - name: "holiday-mode"
    time_intervals:
      - dates: ['2024-12-25', '2024-12-26', '2024-01-01']
```

### 1.6 Alert Tuning

Reduce noise và improve signal.

```yaml
# Alert tuning process
tuning_guidelines:
  # Too many alerts?
  - check: "Alert frequency > 10/day"
    action: "Review if alerts are actionable"
    possible_issues:
      - "Thresholds too sensitive"
      - "Missing for: clause"
      - "No aggregation"

  # Too few alerts?
  - check: "No alerts for days"
    action: "Review coverage"
    possible_gaps:
      - "Missing SLO-based alerts"
      - "No capacity alerts"
      - "Missing dependency alerts"

# Common tuning adjustments
tuning_examples:
  # Add for clause to prevent flapping
  - before: "alert: HighErrorRate\n  expr: error_rate > 0.01"
    after: "alert: HighErrorRate\n  expr: error_rate > 0.01\n  for: 5m"

  # Aggregate to reduce noise
  - before: "alert: HighErrorRate\n  expr: error_rate{instance=~'.+'}"
    after: "alert: HighErrorRate\n  expr: avg(error_rate) by (service) > 0.01"

  # Add severity-appropriate thresholds
  - before: "alert: HighLatency\n  expr: latency > 500"
    after: |
      alert: HighLatencyWarning
        expr: latency > 500
        for: 5m
        labels: { severity: warning }
      alert: HighLatencyCritical
        expr: latency > 2000
        for: 2m
        labels: { severity: critical }
```

### 1.7 Alert Metrics

Track alerting health.

```yaml
# Alert effectiveness metrics
alert_metrics:
  # Volume metrics
  - name: "alerts_total"
    query: sum(rate(alerts_total[5m]))
    description: "Rate of alerts firing"

  - name: "alerts_by_severity"
    query: sum by (severity) (rate(alerts_total[5m]))
    description: "Alerts by severity level"

  # Quality metrics
  - name: "alert_noise_ratio"
    query: |
      sum(rate(alerts_resolved_without_action[5m]))
      / sum(rate(alerts_total[5m]))
    description: "Ratio of noise alerts"

  - name: "mean_time_to_acknowledge"
    query: |
      avg by (alertname) (
        rate(alert_acknowledge_duration_seconds_sum[1h])
        / rate(alert_acknowledge_duration_seconds_count[1h])
      )
    description: "Average time to acknowledge"

  - name: "mean_time_to_resolve"
    query: |
      avg by (alertname) (
        rate(incident_duration_seconds_sum[1h])
        / rate(incident_duration_seconds_count[1h])
      )
    description: "Average time to resolve"

  # SLO metrics
  - name: "error_budget_remaining"
    query: slo:error-budget-remaining:ratio
    description: "Remaining error budget"

  - name: "burn_rate"
    query: slo:current-burn-rate:ratio
    description: "Current error budget burn rate"
```

## 2. Incident Response

### 2.1 Nguyên tắc cơ bản

**1. Blameless Culture**

Focus on systems, not individuals. Learn from incidents. Psychological safety. Continuous improvement.

**2. Clear Roles**

Incident Commander. Technical Lead. Communications Lead. Clear responsibilities. Defined authority.

**3. Structured Response**

Detect. Triage. Respond. Resolve. Review. Clear phases.

**4. Communication**

Internal updates. Customer communication. Status page. Stakeholder updates. Clear channels.

**5. Escalation Path**

Define escalation criteria. Time-based escalation. Severity levels. Clear contacts.

**6. Runbooks**

Pre-defined procedures. Step-by-step guides. Tested regularly. Kept current.

**7. Postmortems**

Blame-free review. Root cause analysis. Action items. Follow-up tracking.

**8. On-Call**

Fair rotation. Reasonable load. Tools and access. Support and compensation.

**9. Severity Levels**

P1: Complete outage. P2: Major degradation. P3: Minor issue. P4: Low impact.

**10. Continuous Improvement**

Track metrics. Identify trends. Improve processes. Update runbooks.

### 2.2 Incident Severity Matrix

| Severity | Description | Response Time | Update Frequency | Example |
|----------|-------------|---------------|-----------------|---------|
| SEV1 | Complete outage affecting all users | Immediate | Every 15 min | API down, payment failed |
| SEV2 | Major feature degraded, >25% users affected | 15 min | Every 30 min | Login broken, checkout failed |
| SEV3 | Minor feature degraded, <25% users affected | 1 hour | Hourly | Slow search, email delayed |
| SEV4 | Cosmetic issue, no user impact | Next business day | Daily | UI bug, minor typo |

Escalation Criteria:
- No progress in 30 min → Escalate
- Multiple teams affected → Escalate
- Customer data at risk → Escalate immediately
- Media/social attention → Escalate immediately

### 2.3 Incident Commander Role

```typescript
// incident-commander responsibilities
interface IncidentCommander {
  // Take command of the incident
  takeCommand(): void;
  
  // Coordinate response
  coordinate(): void;
  
  // Make decisions
  makeDecision(): void;
  
  // Communicate status
  communicate(): void;
  
  // Delegate tasks
  delegate(): void;
  
  // Close incident
  closeIncident(): void;
}

// Responsibilities
// 1. Declare incident severity
// 2. Assemble response team
// 3. Ensure communication
// 4. Make critical decisions
// 5. Authorize escalations
// 6. Declare resolution
// 7. Coordinate postmortem
```

### 2.4 Incident Response Workflow

#### 1. DETECTION (T+0:00)

**Who**: Anyone detecting the issue

**Actions**:
- Verify the issue is real (not false positive)
- Note initial symptoms and impact
- Check if already known/being worked

**Output**:
- Issue confirmed
- Initial severity assessment
- Created incident channel (#inc-YYYY-MM-DD-XXX)

#### 2. TRIAGE (T+0:15)

**Who**: First responder / On-call engineer

**Actions**:
- Assign severity (P1-P4)
- Page incident commander for P1/P2
- Start incident channel
- Begin logging timeline
- Initial investigation

**Communication**:
- Post initial message to #incidents
- Update status page if customer-visible
- Notify stakeholders if P1/P2

**Output**:
- Severity assigned
- IC assigned (P1/P2)
- Response team assembled
- Timeline started

#### 3. RESPONSE (T+0:15 to T+Resolution)

**Who**: Response team led by IC

**Actions**:
- Follow runbooks
- Investigate root cause
- Implement mitigation
- Test fix
- Coordinate across teams
- Regular updates

**Communication**:
- Updates every 15-30 min to #incidents
- Status page updates
- Stakeholder notifications
- Customer comms if needed

**Output**:
- Root cause identified
- Fix implemented
- Service restored

#### 4. RESOLUTION (T+Resolution)

**Who**: IC with team

**Actions**:
- Verify fix is working
- Confirm metrics are normal
- Declare resolution
- Plan follow-up actions

**Communication**:
- Resolution message to #incidents
- Update status page
- Notify stakeholders
- Cancel any active pages

**Output**:
- Incident resolved
- Follow-up items documented
- Postmortem scheduled

#### 5. POSTMORTEM (Within 48-72 hours)

**Who**: Response team + IC + stakeholders

**Actions**:
- Timeline review
- Root cause analysis (5 Whys, Fishbone)
- Impact assessment
- Action items creation
- Process improvements

**Output**:
- Postmortem document
- Action items with owners
- Process improvements
- Updated runbooks

## 3. On-Call Best Practices

### 3.1 On-Call Rotation

Effective on-call practices.

```yaml
# On-call schedule
oncall:
  schedule:
    rotation_type: weekly
    handoff_time: "09:00 UTC"
    participants:
      - name: Alice
        email: alice@example.com
      - name: Bob
        email: bob@example.com
      - name: Charlie
        email: charlie@example.com

  rotations:
    - name: primary
      current: alice
      schedule: weekly
      escalation_delay: 30m

    - name: secondary
      current: bob
      schedule: weekly
      escalation_delay: 1h

  escalation:
    - level: 1
      timeout: 30m
      contacts: [primary_oncall]
    - level: 2
      timeout: 1h
      contacts: [secondary_oncall, team_lead]
    - level: 3
      contacts: [engineering_manager, vp_engineering]

  policies:
    max_hours_per_week: 40
    min_days_between_shifts: 7
    compensation:
      on_call_stipend: $100/day
      incident_response: $50/incident
```

### 3.2 Escalation Policy

```yaml
# escalation.yaml
